# Oldest Alembic revision this build is able to start against.
#
# The orchestrator reads this (via the image's OCI labels) and refuses to
# upgrade a town whose observed schema is outside
# [min_db_revision .. db_revision]. It is the expand/contract contract:
#
#   * Purely ADDITIVE migration (expand) — leave this at the PREVIOUS head. The
#     new build still runs on the old schema, so the town can be started on the
#     new image and migrated with no window where code and schema disagree.
#
#   * DESTRUCTIVE migration (contract: column/table dropped or renamed, NOT NULL
#     added, type narrowed) — set this to the NEW head. The new build cannot run
#     on the old schema, so the orchestrator must see the town already migrated
#     and will otherwise fail the canary rather than serve 500s.
#
# Blank or missing means "the revision immediately before head", which is the
# expand case. Get it wrong in the safe direction: naming a NEWER revision only
# blocks a rollout, while naming an older one lets a broken build reach towns.
# Head is a8c6e2f4b9d1 (platform_feedback_module) -- a new table plus a nullable
# column, purely additive with nothing existing touched, so the rule above puts
# this at the revision immediately before it: f6b4d8e2a3c5
# (registration_prompt_dismissed), itself additive.
#
# It had once been left at c3d4e5f6a7b8 for fourteen revisions, several of them
# contract migrations (retire_hard_delete, drop_dead_documents_pushed_flag, the
# NOT NULL on retention state) that this build genuinely cannot start against --
# so the declared floor was claiming compatibility the image does not have.
a8c6e2f4b9d1
